  "text": "Berkeley's nineteen seventy-three graduate admissions are the famous case. Here's the pattern in two departments. Overall, fifty-five percent of the men get in, and only thirty-three percent of the women. Now split by department. In the easy one, women do better, sixty-five percent to sixty. In the hard one, women do better again, twenty-seven and a half to twenty-five. Ahead in both departments... and behind overall. How? Six hundred of the seven hundred women applied to the hard one. Six hundred of the men, to the easy one. That's Simpson's paradox. A lurking variable, here the department, sat unevenly across the groups. So before you trust a total, ask what it's averaging over. And a lurking variable can flip a slope, too.\n\nHere's weekly exercise against blood pressure, for a hundred and four people. One line through everyone rises, about nine points for every extra hour of exercise a week. Now color them by age. Inside every age band, the line falls. Older people here exercise more, and have higher blood pressure. Age drives both. It's a confounder. The cure is a randomized experiment. If a coin decides who exercises, age can't. Sometimes, though, there's no third variable at all. Just luck.",
